A||||8||Denny||Need expert help with scan readings||||5||
Z||000000||Denny||09-03-2001||12:02 PM||||Hey all! I'm trying to eliminate an annoying problem with my 1986 GT 4 speed: When cold (morning cold) I get bucking when trying to start out. After the car warms up I get what feels like a low-load miss (only way I can describe it). When cruising (after the engine is warmed up) with light or no load, the engine occasionally stumbles like it's missing, but runs great otherwise. Lots of pull, and runs great at most RPM, or any speed over 40 in high gear.<P>All ignition components are new, as well as new egr valve and tube (replaced a cracked tube).<P>The following table is from scan tool readings, I did both cars for comparison. The only things I can spot (both on GT side, are the low (relative) IAC step counts, and then high O2 sensor reading, both of which I suspect are caused by a slight vacuum leak.  Comments??????<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/scan_tool_data.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Denny (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Joe Torma||09-03-2001||01:12 PM||||Check your TPS again at idle on your GT...way too high.  Set around .54V.<P>Start there.<P>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-03-2001||01:33 PM||||Check the TPS arm like he said. also make sure the connector is clean and free of damage.<P>Then check for vac leaks and things.<P>Keep in mind some of those numbers, like O2 counts and block learn are dynamic. They change over time. Example, the longer the engine runs the higher the cross counts will be. <P>Rich/lean flag should also change pretty  constantly while the car is running.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||09-04-2001||10:34 AM||||Denny, I just resolved my intermittant mis/stumble with light or no load.  The symptoms were identical to yours except I have an auto.  Yours could have different root causes but you may want to see how I cured mine.  Anyway, I replaced my injectors and rebuilt the fuel rail (regulator mostly) and changed the O2 sensor.  The reason I did this is that when I had the heads off for the head rebuild (1000 mi ago), I noticed that the inectors were pretty wet around the caps w/ a lot of carbon buildup.  They had 156K on them at that point.  I had another set from a car w/ 105K that I sent to Marren Auotsport for cleaning and eval. He could only get three of the 6 in range so I went with new or slightly used.  The O2 sensor I replaced because the previous owner had knocked out the unleaded gas opening in the filler neck and the cat was totally burned out.  I previously replaced the cat about 800 mi ago.  While I was working on it this time I took the plugs out for a plug read and found that in 1000 miles three went bad (see the poot-poot post later when I update it) so I changed them.  That was due to detonation I think.  In addition, I bought a vacuum check pump/gauge and checked everything. I had to bypass the EGR solenoid/switch and evaporation canister as they were defective and causing vacuum leaks.  Anyway you may want to check the injectors.  In looking at your scan data I would agree that the TPS 'looks' a little high but won't cause a problem as long as the voltage is stable for any given throttle opening.  The computer reads initial TPS when you turn the key on and uses that for idle until a lower voltage is read and that would only happen if the switch is bad.  Your IAC opening does look low indicating a vacuum leak (see above) and block learn high.  My readings when I look a my car while I had the intermittant mis also showed those and the slightly higher O2.  It's hard to rely on the O2 reading but mine was slightly higher than a friends when we compared.  Injector puls width readings only make sense when reasonble good injectors are pulsing so that reading may be indicative of a problem.  Sorry this is so long but your symptoms matched mine, I thought I would let you know what I went through to cure it. GL and email or post me a message if you have specific questions.  Oh my car is an '87 GT BTW.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 09-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||skitime||09-04-2001||10:50 AM||||Denny, you were my expert.  Now I am lost without a technical advisor. hehe  Hey I finally fixed Voronica's hard starting and a similar low-load miss on her 88.  I replaced the injectors.  It starts like a champ and purrs like a kitten now.  Injectors???? maybe?<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||09-04-2001||02:36 PM||||Hey that looks like 2 for the injectors on the low load mis-fire symptom.  Thanks Skitime!||||reg||1
Z||000006||XDrewX||09-04-2001||06:05 PM||||I have this same miss,  but mine did not start until I replaced the fuel filter and ran some Fuel injector cleaner through engine.  I have a new O2 sensor too.  <BR>My engine only has 60k on it but before I recently bought it, the car was only driven 1000 miles in 2 years.  I never had a miss until i performed the above items.<P>Could the injectors get clogged just from the engine sitting a long time ????||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||09-04-2001||06:27 PM||||XDrew - The cleaner may have loosened hardened desposits that won't go thru the injector screen.  If your car only has 60K on it (including injectors) then take them out and send them to Marren Motorsport to be cleaned spray pattern checked and characterized.  They will run like new injectors.  Check out thier website @ <A HREF="http://www.injector.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.injector.com/</A>  BTW he told me that injector cleaner if used too much or not well diluted will eat the wire coating off of the coils.  GL<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 09-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||09-04-2001||07:03 PM||||Sorry, I'm not much help, but what software/hardware combo are you using?||||reg||5
